ttachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","name":"Wannabe Demon Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-27T19:51:28-07:00","id":1134716,"title":"","content":"Sacerdos is totally right that I can pass over some of the sources I have been looking at Wyandot mythology with. Helpfully, the bigger collections of their texts are digitized, and free for people to access. The older of the two, <i>Huron and Wyandot Mythology, With an Appendix Containing Earlier Published Records</i> was put together in 1915 by C. M. Barbeau as part of an anthropological series being done by the Canadian Department of Mines at the time. Since it is in public domain, it can be found on the Internet Archive right <a href=\"https://archive.org/stream/huronwyandotmyth00barb#page/n5/mode/2up\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>. As always with a document this old, it is a product of its time, anthropology was undergoing some growing pains, so keep that in mind when reading it. The second free collection is actually hosted by the Wyandot Nation themselves, a transcribed collection named <i>Indian Myths</i> by a William Elsey Connelly, who was a member of the Wyandot tribe (by adoption). It can be found <a href=\"http://www.wyandotte-nation.org/culture/folk-lore-and-myths/indian-myths/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>.<br /> <br /> I don't know if you have access to these articles, but these are also rather interesting despite their age. If you want to dig into these ones, check out libraries around you, I bet someone has access to the articles since they're so old.<div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Hale, Horatio. &quot;Huron Folk-Lore. I. Cosmogonic Myths. The Good and Evil Minds.&quot; The Journal of American Folklore 1, no. 3 (October - December 1888): 177-83.<br /> Hale, Horatio. &quot;Huron Folk-Lore. II. The Story of Tijaiha, the Sorcerer.&quot; The Journal of American Folklore 2, no. 7 (1889): 249-54.<br /> Hale, Horatio. &quot;Huron Folk-Lore. III. The Legend of the Thunderers.&quot; The Journal of American Folklore 4, no. 15 (October - December 1891): 289-94.<br /> Barbeau, C. M. &quot;Supernatural Beings of the Huron and Wyandot.&quot; American Anthropologist, New Series, 16, no. 2 (1914): 288-313.</div>Sadly, for the rest of the cultures you are looking into, I can't be much help with directly. I can give you some suggestions for how to track stuff down if you want. If there are any Universities near where you are, totally just wander into their library and find one of their database computers. Most Universities will have programs so you can get a library card even if you are not a student, faculty, or alumni, so you can take books out no problem. Universities sort of just collect stuff over time, they will have something on the Pantheons you are looking for. Only thing to look out for his how old, and reputable the things you are reading are. If you can't do this, the other option is to find a Professor in the specific field you want to look at, and just drop them an email. Or see if you can find syllabuses for courses taught on these cultural groups floating around on line to mine for reading lists.<br /> <br /> Secondly, for the First Nations you want to look into, check out their websites. Just like the Wyandot I linked to up there, the Tribes will normally have their own websites, and the ones I have visited tend to have a section on cultural things where you could find mythology.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-28T16:07:30-07:00","id":1134986,"title":"","content":"The Romani don't have a pantheon as far as I'm aware, they were originally Hindu and then were converted to Christianity and/or Islam, which lead them to have some distinct cultural views, but the vast majority as far as I'm aware are monotheistic. They have plenty of interesting folklore (like vampire watermelons) that could be incorporated and a patron saint (St. Sarah) who was probably originally the goddess Kali, so there's plenty to play around with, but no pantheon. Sacred-texts has a Roma section with a few Romani folktales if you wanna check them out.<br /> <br /> The Lusitanians are in a similar boat to the Gauls, only they're even more obscure. All we have are inscriptions and the occasional statue, so there really isn't much to go on. Try looking into books about Celtic mythology in general. I haven't found any that focus specifically on the Lusitanians, at least not in English, but if you can read Portuguese or Spanish you might have better luck.<br /> <br /> Also for the Native American pantheons, native-languages.org has a collection of different stories divided up by tribe, so you might find a few there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13472-~nkf~","name":"~NKF~","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"25"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-29T07:28:35-07:00","id":1135148,"title":"","content":"I have spent some considerable amount of time researching the Blackfoot gods, and spirituality. i have found that while there are similarities, the Native American tribes each had their own version of how the cosmos worked. not to mention that at some point, they began to refer to their main deity (Old Man) as the Sun, and their trickster as Old Man (Napi). I wish i could be more helpful but i have found this site to be helpful <a href=\"http://www.firstpeople.us/FP-Html-Legends/Legends-AB.html#Blackfoot\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.firstpeople.us/FP-Html-Le...html#Blackfoot</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12529-nico-diangelo","name":"Nico diAngelo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12529_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"20"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}